> > a. Forward references in prep commits: allow them within a series,
> >    as git does (my recommendation), or keep the ban?
>
> I think this is probably okay. Give me an example of what you mean
> though. I think this is the "Prepare for..." language?

Yes, that is it. Here is git's own example, commit f7aeeae66
"builtin/clone: move around `setup_reference()`":

    In a subsequent commit, `setup_reference()` will start to call
    `copy_alternates()`. Prepare for this by moving the function
    further down so that we can avoid adding a declaration.

The commit only moves a function. Without the first sentence, a
reviewer sees churn with no reason. With it, the reviewer knows what
the move is for and can check that the move suits that purpose.

In this series it would look like this, if the check in step 6 needs
format-patch.sh to hand it each file:

    libexec/mail: Collect the files format-patch.sh writes

    format-patch.sh adds the change id to each file as git writes
    it, and keeps no list of the files. A later commit in this
    series checks every message before any of them is posted, so it
    needs the whole list first. Collect the file names, then add the
    change ids in a second loop. No functional change.

What the rule would allow is "a later commit in this series". What
it would still ban is pointing at work that is not in the series:
"a follow-up will...", or a patch not yet sent. The series lands
whole, so the reference always resolves in the tree.

