[...]
> Before we get into this, give me some feedback on this approach -
> specifically whether tests and docs should go together, or be
> separate. If separate, which one comes first.

Separate passes, with docs first. My reasons:

- Docs are the go/no-go gate you described. If a pass of docs shows
  you would not use the tool, the plan changes, and any tests already
  written against it are wasted. Docs are also the cheapest layer for
  you to review, so the cheap decision comes first.
- Tests then check the docs. Each claim in the docs becomes a test.
  A claim that cannot be tested is too vague, and writing the test is
  what finds that. A test that matches no claim points at behavior
  the docs left out. Doing them in one pass hides both, because the
  writer bends each one to fit the other.
- Each pass gives you one kind of thing to review: first "is this
  the tool I want", then "is this how we will know it works". Mixed
  in one pass, the docs review tends to become a test review.

The passes are layers inside each commit, not commits of their own.
After all of them, each commit holds its message, docs, tests, and
code together, which is rule 7. Some commits skip a layer. A prep
commit like the example above has no docs and relies on the existing
tests; a commit that is all prose, like CLAUDE.md's pointer, has no
tests.

Three things to settle before I start:

1. The tests layer goes red until the code lands. In my workspace
   that is fine, since only the finished series is posted, and each
   posted commit has its tests and code together. A failing middle
   state is the point of writing the test first.

2. A plan of empty commits cannot be posted as [PATCH]. am.sh runs
   git am with --empty=drop, and just fetch abandons other people's
   empty commits, so the plan would vanish when applied. I propose
   each layer goes out as an [RFC PATCH] round in this thread: the
   plan as message-only mail, then the docs layer as patches (they
   are non-empty from then on), then the full series as [PATCH] in
   a new thread. You review each layer before I add the next.

3. In this series only step 6, the check, has tests to write.
   libexec/mail has no tests today, so that layer also adds a small
   test script: sample messages the check must accept and ones it
   must refuse. Steps 1, 2, 3 and 5 are docs only, so for them the
   method is message, then docs. That is a fair first trial, but a
   light one for the tests layer. If you want a fuller trial of it,
   the next piece of code work would suit better.

If that sounds right, my next message is the plan: the message-only
